"""Generate results, methods, caption, and coverage summaries from offline statistics."""
import json
from collections import Counter
from extract import BASE,DATA,ARMS,SOURCES,FAMILIES,LABELS,write_json
NAMES=dict(zip(SOURCES,['ASI-Bench','BixBench','DiscoveryBench','EXP-Bench','SciConvBench']));NAMES.update(dict(zip(FAMILIES,['Life sciences','Physical / engineering','Math / CS','Social / humanities'])))
def table(head,rows):return '\n'.join(['| '+' | '.join(head)+' |','|'+'---|'*len(head)]+['| '+' | '.join(map(str,r))+' |' for r in rows])
def cell(r,counts=True):
 if r['mean'] is None:return '—'
 ci=r['ci95'];text=f"{r['mean']:.2f}"+(f" [{ci[0]:.2f}, {ci[1]:.2f}]" if ci else ' [CI unavailable]')
 return text+(f"; n={r['tasks']}, g={r['studies']}" if counts else '')
def pick(rows,**kw):
 rr=[r for r in rows if all(r.get(k)==v for k,v in kw.items())];assert len(rr)==1,(kw,len(rr));return rr[0]
def main():
 s=json.loads((DATA/'summary.json').read_text());snap=json.loads((DATA/'snapshot.json').read_text())
 cover=[]
 for a,label in zip(ARMS,LABELS):
  score=[r for r in snap['scores'] if r['arm']==a];traces=[r for r in snap['traces'] if r['arm']==a];rec=[r for r in snap['recovery'] if r['arm']==a and r['recovery'] is not None]
  cover.append([label,','.join(map(str,sorted({r['trial'] for r in score}))),len(score),sum(r['score'] is not None for r in score),sum(r.get('exclusion') is None for r in traces),len(rec)])
 within=pick(s['repetition'],persona_i=ARMS[0],persona_j=ARMS[0],variant='primary',level='all',group='all');between=s['repetition_overview']['between_all_pairs'];diff=s['repetition_overview']['between_minus_within_off']
 lines=['# Four-metric offline suite','', '[Executed notebook](metric_suite.ipynb) · [Main figure](figures/metric_suite.pdf) · [Candidate caption](FIGURE_CAPTION.md)','',
 'Selected settings: off/155/001/003/046. All measurements use original Astra-low endpoints from the historical protocol. Saved traces, native judgments and native scores only; no new LLM inference. Earlier repaired-endpoint performance figures remain separate. This suite is exploratory and does not evaluate human realism.','',
 '## Current figure revision','', 'Panel A now measures exact four-word repetition within conversations for both simulator and actor outputs. See [the current word-repetition report](WORD_REPETITION.md) for its definition, results, coverage, role-specific distributions and sensitivities. Earlier cross-run structural results below remain historical supplementary analysis and are not the current A. B now shows complete-fact recovery (all annotated facts recovered in a run), not the partial recovered-fact fraction; D is individual-setting SciConv coverage. The previous B is archived under revisions/v3_partial_recovery/.','', '## Earlier structural-analysis findings','',f"- Structural repetition within off: {cell(within,False)}%. Across all ten persona pairs: {cell(between,False)}%. Their paired difference is {cell(diff,False)} percentage points. Equal source weights; same 100-question panel (77 source-study clusters).",
 '- Structural matches remain common. In SciConv, every observed same-length comparison matches exactly: this native-event metric captures questioning length/stopping, not semantic templates or different scientific methods.',
 '- Native fact recovery is available for all 20 disambiguation cases in every selected setting. Absolute means and same-trial paired contrasts differ for single-trial settings because off has three trials.',
 '- BixBench has identical equal-budget failed-task coverage for off and the persona mixture. SciConv has a positive coverage point difference at three episodes, but its paired interval includes zero. No significant gain or equivalence conclusion is asserted.','',
 '## Coverage','',table(['Setting','Observed trials','Original slots','Evaluable scores','Complete traces','Recovery episodes'],cover),'',
 'Repetition requires ≥2 complete traces for off/155/001 and ≥1 for 003/046 on each included question. It retains 100/200 selected questions: '+', '.join(f"{NAMES[k]} {v}" for k,v in Counter(r['source'] for r in s['repetition_coverage']).items())+'. No trace subsampling. Within-persona diagonals for 003/046 are unavailable.','',
 '![Four metrics](figures/metric_suite.png)','',
 '## 1. Interaction-pattern repetition','',
 'The canonical sequence retains ordered simulator review decisions, additional actor handoffs and end/limit markers. Initial requests and each phase’s first mandatory actor handoff are omitted; actual decisions on mandatory reviews are retained because their outcomes can vary. All natural-language prose and transport/retry events are ignored. SciConv retains questions, replies, final specification and cap/complete termination. Repeated events are not collapsed. End-state markers are protocol outcomes, not scientific success labels.',
 'For each question, between-persona repetition is the fraction of all cross-trial pairs with identical sequences; within-persona repetition uses only distinct repeat pairs. Average within source then equally over five sources. Replicate pairs are not independent sampling units. The same-length sensitivity conditions on equal exchange counts and has different pair/question coverage. It is exported rather than silently replacing the primary metric.','',
 table(['Pair (row / column)','Repetition % [95% CI]; questions, studies','Δ from within-off repetition (pp)'],[[f"{LABELS[ARMS.index(r['persona_i'])]} / {LABELS[ARMS.index(r['persona_j'])]}",cell(r),'—' if r['persona_i']==r['persona_j'] else cell(pick(s['repetition_delta_vs_off'],persona_i=r['persona_i'],persona_j=r['persona_j'],variant='primary',level='all',group='all'))] for r in s['repetition'] if r['variant']=='primary' and r['level']=='all']),'',
 '[Source breakdown](figures/repetition_by_benchmark.pdf) · [All source/family cells and same-length sensitivity](data/repetition.csv) · [Exact canonical sequences](data/trace_patterns.csv)','',
 '## 2. Native missing-fact recovery','',
 'Disambiguation only. Read the saved native fraction, validate against n_items, average repeats within case then cases equally. This is endpoint fact recovery before the intent gate; not disclosure, incorporation, or per-turn information gain. Reuse original saved judge responses; no regrading. The strict new SciConv protocol has no scored data and is excluded.','',
 table(['Setting','Recovery % [95% CI]; cases, studies','Matched Δ vs off (pp)'],[[l,cell(pick(s['outcomes'],metric='fact_recovery',arm=a,level='all',group='all')),'Reference' if a==ARMS[0] else cell(pick(s['contrasts'],metric='fact_recovery',persona_i=a,persona_j=ARMS[0],level='all',group='all'))] for a,l in zip(ARMS,LABELS)]),'',
 'All five settings share 20 cases; the common-case sensitivity equals the available-case recovery means. Off/155/001 have 60 episodes each, 003/046 have 20 each. Paired deltas for single-trial personas use off trial 1 only. Current CIs resample source-study clusters, whereas the older Experiment 08 report stratified by domain; means agree but intervals need not.','',
 '## 3. Native scientific outcomes','',
 'Native source scores are unchanged: ASI normalized native score; Bix correctness; Discovery final_score; EXP gated_coverage_mean with documented initial fallback; SciConv FRR. Scientific missing submissions are zero; infrastructure failures are missing. Absolute ASI values mix historical judge configurations and are descriptive. All pairwise contrasts enforce native evaluator compatibility, including corrected ASI judges on both sides. Super-families are descriptive mixtures of different native metrics.','']
 for field,groups in [('source',SOURCES),('family',FAMILIES)]:
  lines.extend(['### '+('Benchmark' if field=='source' else 'Super-family'),'',table(['Setting',*[NAMES[g] for g in groups]],[[l,*[cell(pick(s['outcomes'],metric='native_score',arm=a,level=field,group=g)) for g in groups]] for a,l in zip(ARMS,LABELS)]),''])
 lines.extend(['[Native score figure with CIs](figures/native_scores_with_ci.pdf) · [All compatible pairwise contrasts](data/matched_contrasts.csv)','',
 '## 4. Equal-budget failed-task coverage','',
 'Use native binary Bix correctness and SciConv FRR. A scientific failure is score=0, including a scientific missing submission. No threshold is invented for ASI/Discovery/EXP. Fixed common questions require off trials 1–3 and at least one valid original result for each of the four personas, with compatible evaluator versions. This retains 26 Bix and 40 SciConv questions. Exclusion reasons are exported.',
 'At budget m=1,2,3, average every size-m subset of the three observed off trials. For the persona mixture, choose m distinct personas uniformly from the four, and choose one available original trial per chosen persona uniformly. The reported failure coverage is the expected fraction of questions with at least one failure under these empirical allocation policies; it is not an optimized persona selection or an independent new rollout estimate. Different trial availability is part of this conditional policy, not eliminated by matching budgets.','',
 table(['Benchmark','Episodes/question','Repeated off %','Persona mixture %','Paired Δ (pp)'],[[NAMES[src],m,*[cell(pick(s['failure_coverage'],variant='primary',level='source',group=src,budget=m,strategy=st)) for st in ['off','mixture','difference']]] for src in ['bixbench','sciconvbench'] for m in [1,2,3]]),'',
 'The mixture-only and off-only failed-task sets are averaged over independently sampled allocation choices between the two strategies; they are not distinct failure-mechanism counts. Their difference equals the coverage difference. Curves are nested budget summaries on a fixed question panel, not independent observations. Additional budget gains can be obtained by adjacent-budget subtraction; no attribution to an individual persona is claimed.',
 'A supplementary SciConv sensitivity keeps only questions whose saved intent-capture flag is 1 in every included episode. This is outcome-conditioned and not the primary analysis. It checks one native fidelity signal, not full simulator factual correctness. Its denominators are in the CSV.','',
 '## Uncertainty and limitations','',
 '20,000 percentile bootstrap draws over source-study clusters, carrying all matched outcomes within each study. Average repeats within task first. Source weights are fixed: equal source weights for aggregate repetition, observed task proportions elsewhere. Paired-difference CIs use per-question differences, not subtraction of marginal CI endpoints. CIs are pointwise, conditional on saved trials and valid coverage, with no significance stars or multiplicity-adjusted claims. One-study strata contribute no resampling variation; total-one-study cells have unavailable CIs. Per-cell source/study counts are exported. Original persona selection and the selected five-setting panel were exploratory.',
 'No new semantic labels, human-realism judgments, embeddings, actor/simulator calls, or task executions were generated. Patterns are coarse protocol structure. Unequal repeat counts and incomplete original episodes limit generalization. Larger failure coverage is informative only under valid task-preserving simulation; the saved intent check is incomplete evidence of fidelity.','',
 '## Reproduction','', '`OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 .venv/bin/python Experiment/09_offline_metric_suite/code/extract.py`\n\n`OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 .venv/bin/python Experiment/09_offline_metric_suite/code/analyze.py`\n\n`MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/psb-mpl OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 .venv/bin/python Experiment/09_offline_metric_suite/code/build_notebook.py`','',
 'The snapshot stores only numeric metadata, protocol action labels and file hashes/paths, not private conversation text. Original files remain authoritative. `code/test_metrics.py` checks canonicalization, distinct-repeat pairing, exhaustive allocation arithmetic, task weighting, and equality to previously saved outcome/recovery means.'])
 lines.extend(['', '## Previous revision: structural A / violin B / individual D', '',
 'Native scientific performance (former panel C) is omitted from this figure because it is reported separately. The original four-panel export and notebook are archived under revisions/v1_four_panel/. Panel letters A/B/D are retained for continuity during revision.', '',
 '**A — reading the heatmap.** A value of 83.0 means that 83.0% of cross-setting episode pairs for the same question had exactly the same canonical event sequence, after averaging within benchmark and then equally over benchmarks. High values mean more structural repetition; this is not a semantic-distance or significance heatmap. The off/off diagonal (85.2%) is repetition across distinct off trials. Single-trial 003/046 diagonals are unavailable, not zero.', '',
 '**B — reading the violins.** Each colored dot is one case mean over available original trials (20 cases per setting). The violin is a Scott-bandwidth Gaussian KDE evaluated only between observed minimum and maximum values; width represents relative density, with each violin scaled to the same maximum width. Dots expose discreteness and ceiling values. The white diamond is the mean and the black bar its pointwise 95% study-cluster CI. The violin itself is not a confidence interval. Repeats are averaged before plotting so settings with three trials do not get triple weight.', '',
 '**D — each setting separately.** SciConv only, same 40 common questions for every setting and budget. Within a setting, average the indicator of at least one failure over every size-m subset of its available trials, then average questions. Off/155/001 have m=1,2,3; 003/046 only m=1. No extrapolation, duplicate trials, or sampling with replacement. Lines/bands represent means/pointwise 95% CIs; single-trial settings have a marker/error bar. Higher values mean finding failures on more questions, not higher task success. This within-setting comparison replaces the mixture in the main display; mixture tables above remain as supplementary analysis.', '',
 table(['Setting','Episodes/question','SciConv failed-task coverage % [95% CI]'],[[LABELS[ARMS.index(r['arm'])],r['budget'],cell(r)] for r in s['persona_failure_coverage']]), '',
 '[Individual-setting source data](data/persona_failure_coverage.csv) · [Per-question allocation expectations](data/persona_failure_questions.csv) · [Recovery case means](data/recovery_case_means.csv)'])
 lines.extend(['', '## Current B: complete-fact recovery', '',
 'For each saved disambiguation run, score 100 if facts_recovered equals facts_total and 0 otherwise. Average within case across available trials, then equally over the 20 cases. This is all-or-nothing factual completeness before intent gating; it is not native intent-gated FRR and does not represent a newly harder task or protocol. Original partial-recovery statistics above are retained as a complementary measure.', '',
 table(['Setting','Complete recovery % [95% CI]','Matched change vs off (pp)'],[[label,cell(pick(s['outcomes'],metric='complete_fact_recovery',arm=arm,level='all',group='all')),'Reference' if arm==ARMS[0] else cell(pick(s['contrasts'],metric='complete_fact_recovery',persona_i=arm,persona_j=ARMS[0],level='all',group='all'))] for arm,label in zip(ARMS,LABELS)]), '',
 'Violin dots are the 20 case-level proportions: 0/33.3/66.7/100% for three-trial settings and 0/100% for single-trial settings. KDEs are descriptive smoothing of these discrete proportions, not a continuous underlying measurement or CI; observed dots are shown. White diamonds and black bars are equal-case means and 95% study-cluster bootstrap CIs. Single-trial matched differences use off trial 1 and need not equal differences from the three-trial off absolute mean. A now includes both simulator and actor messages; D is unchanged. No new inference was performed.', '',
 '[Current B source data](data/complete_recovery_case_means.csv)'])
 (BASE/'REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
 word=json.loads((DATA/'word_repetition_summary.json').read_text())
 sim_n=next(r['questions'] for r in word['coverage'] if r['role']=='simulator' and r['width']==4)
 actor_n=next(r['questions'] for r in word['coverage'] if r['role']=='actor' and r['width']==4)
 caption=f'''# Candidate manuscript caption

**Word-based self-repetition, complete-fact recovery and failure coverage under five simulator settings.** Off, 155, 001, 003 and 046 use saved original Astra-low episodes; off/155/001 have three trials and 003/046 one. **A**, fraction of overlapping four-word sequences in a later message that appeared in an earlier same-role message of that conversation, averaged over eligible turns, trials within question, questions within benchmark and then equally across benchmarks. Filled circles represent simulator messages ({sim_n} common questions); hollow squares represent actor messages ({actor_n} common questions). Bars are pointwise 95% CIs. Case-normalized Unicode words are used; Han text is dictionary-segmented with jieba (HMM disabled). Initial/context messages, code fences and Markdown blockquotes are excluded. At least two length-eligible messages are required; role-specific panels can differ. This measures verbatim phrase reuse, not judged semantic redundancy or mode collapse. **B**, complete-fact recovery on 20 SciConv disambiguation cases, before intent gating. Dots are within-case percentages of runs recovering every annotated missing fact; violins smooth these discrete proportions with a Scott-bandwidth KDE restricted to observed ranges. White diamonds/bars show equal-case means/95% CIs. **D**, expected percentage of 40 common SciConv questions with at least one scientific failure among m episodes of each setting, averaging all size-m subsets without replacement. Off/155/001 support budgets 1–3; 003/046 only 1. Bands/error bars show 95% CIs. All CIs use 20,000 source-study bootstrap draws conditional on saved trials. Higher failure coverage is not higher task success. Different panels have different eligible populations.

## Agent handoff

The current A uses four-word matching, replacing the earlier 40-character version at the user's request. Read WORD_REPETITION.md for tokenization, matching, coverage, CIs and one-/two-/three-word sensitivity. Single-word reuse is vocabulary reuse and should not be interpreted as stagnation. The definition was revised after inspecting character-based results; all analyses are exploratory. Preserve the old character figures under revisions/v5_character_matching/ and numeric results under data/self_repetition_*. Word data are under data/word_repetition_*. No stemming, semantic matching, translation or model inference was added. Language and conversation-length effects remain. B/D are unchanged from the previous figure; B is complete rather than partial recovery and D must not extrapolate single-trial settings. Panel identifiers remain A/B/D until final manuscript numbering is settled.
'''
 (BASE/'FIGURE_CAPTION.md').write_text(caption)
 write_json(BASE/'status.json',dict(status='extracted_and_analyzed',inference_calls=0,original_slots=len(snap['scores']),complete_traces=sum(r.get('exclusion') is None for r in snap['traces']),recovery_episodes=sum(r['recovery'] is not None for r in snap['recovery']),repetition_questions=len(s['repetition_coverage']),failure_panel=dict(Counter(r['source'] for r in s['failure_panel'])),notebook='metric_suite.ipynb',report='REPORT.md'))
 print('Report, caption, and status written.',flush=True)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
